{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And the people strove with Moses.</b> In all the complaints we do not find the expression מְרִיבָה except in the matter of water, twice: here, and at Massah and Meribah (Exodus 17:7) — because from water strife was created, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Beresheet Rabbah 4:6): why was ״כִּי טוֹב״ not said on the second day? Because in it strife was created, as it says (Genesis 1:6): ״וִיהִי מַבְדִּיל בֵּין מַיִם לָמָיִם״, and it is explicit in the received tradition (Proverbs 17:14): ״פּוֹטֵר מַיִם רֵאשִׁית מָדוֹן״ — that the waters were freed and separated, one from the other, is רֵאשִׁית מָדוֹן, the beginning of strife, and the prelude to every quarrel. And so too the quarrel of Isaac's shepherds concerning the waters, for all this follows by nature from the primordial waters. And they strove with Moses, who was drawn from the water and whose hand prevailed over the water at the Sea of Reeds and at the Rock — why should he not bring forth for us from the rock of flint now as well?" "And as for what they said, ״וְלוּ גָוַעְנוּ בִּגְוַע אַחֵינוּ״ — they did not say this about death by thirst, for if so, why did they conclude their words with ״וּמַיִם אַיִן לִשְׁתּוֹת״? Rather, the beginning of their words is another and greater complaint: for it had been decreed upon them ״בַּמִּדְבָּר הַזֶּה יִתַּמּוּ וְשָׁם יָמוּתוּ״, and our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Sanhedrin 110b): ״וְשָׁם יָמוּתוּ״ — in the World to Come, for the dead of the wilderness have no portion in the World to Come. And there are those who say that it was regarding this that they said here: ״לָמוּת שָׁם אֲנַחְנוּ וּבְעִירֵנוּ״ — 'we' is equivalent to 'our beasts,' just as the spirit of the beast descends below, so too our spirit. And I shall add a fine teaching: therefore they prefaced by saying ״וְלוּ גָוַעְנוּ בִּגְוַע אַחֵינוּ לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — they likened themselves to 'our brethren, the house of Israel' who died before the decree of ״וְשָׁם יָמוּתוּ״ regarding the World to Come, for then we would have been equal to our brethren in the portion of the World to Come; ״וְלָמָּה הֲבֵאתֶם״ and so forth ״לָמוּת שָׁם אֲנַחְנוּ״ — equivalent to 'our beasts,' for it is the opposite of ״בִּגְוַע אַחֵינוּ״ but rather like the death of our beasts. And through this it is settled why they first used the expression גְּוִיעָה and afterward the expression מִיתָה: for גְּוִיעָה is said of the righteous (Bava Batra 16b), while מִיתָה applies even to the lowly. And this is a proof that concerning the גְּוִיעָה of the righteous, who have a portion in the World to Come, they said this. And when they said ״לָמוּת שָׁם אֲנַחְנוּ וּבְעִירֵנוּ״, they meant: shall we die as the vile beast dies, like the rest of the animals? And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:12.552545802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}